## Changes applied to the vendored code

Kept in `patches/` so they can be replayed onto a newer upstream snapshot
(`git apply -p1 --directory=vendor patches/*.patch`), and submitted upstream as
[PR #7](https://github.com/lenovo1996/KiroCrew-OpenAI-Compatible/pull/7):

1. `stream()` accepts `**kwargs` — KiroCrew 0.11 calls
   `provider.stream(message, allow_image=...)`, which used to raise
   `TypeError` before any token was requested.
2. `stream()` awaits `_fetch_tools_async()` before reading the tool table — the
   synchronous `tool_definitions()` only *schedules* discovery inside a running
   loop and returned `[]`, so the first turn of every session had no tools.
3. `set_model()` added (plus `_bare_model()` to strip the `<provider>/` prefix) —
   without it the dashboard's model picker was silently ignored.

## What this project adds on top

- `bootstrap/` — the injection hook (`hub_boot.pth` + `hub_boot.py` +
  `sitecustomize.py`), the config resolver (`provider_config.py`), and the
  single-instance tool-executor cache.
- `install.ps1` / `install.sh` / `doctor.ps1` / `doctor.sh`.
- Documentation, tests, and the measured numbers in `README.md`.

## Not included

KiroCrew itself. It is proprietary software; this project only writes two small
files into its Python environment at install time and removes them on uninstall.
